{
  "summary": "This study investigates cross-scale decoupling strategies in chiral tensegrity metamaterials to resolve trade-offs between deformation capacity, stiffness, and durability. The researchers designed a chiral three-rod tensegr. cell that mechanically decouples macroscopic shape changes from local strain, achieving 97° torsion with minimal force degradation over 10,000 cycles. Kinematic decoupling at the assembly scale enables rotationally constrained interfaces while maintaining pure translation. The framework demonstrates potential for integrating metamaterial physics with robotic systems in constrained environments.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Mechanical decoupling at unit-cell scale achieves 97° torsion with only 4.87% peak-force degradation after 10,000 cycles",
    "Kinematic decoupling localizes torsional deformation as a boundary-decoupled internal mode",
    "Post-fabrication programmability of stiffness and strain-normalized twist ratio via geometric interlocking"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a cross-scale decoupling framework that resolves fundamental trade-offs in chiral metamaterials, enabling robust torsional deformation and interface compatibility for applications in constrained robotic systems."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated how genomic instability in BRCA2-mutated prostate cancer affects tumor immunity. Using an isogenic model, researchers found BRCA2 loss activated the cGAS/STING pathway, causing immunosuppression via CCR8⁺ regulatory T cells and Ccl8-expressing macrophages. Anti-PD-1 therapy exacerbated immunosone suppression, but CCR8⁺ Treg depletion reversed resistance, with human data confirming elevated CCR8 in BRCA2-mutated tumors, suggesting CCR8⁺ Tregs as a target for immunotherapy.",
  "key_findings": [
    "BRCA2 deficiency activates cGAS/STING pathway, inducing immunosuppression",
    "CCR8⁺ regulatory T cells and Ccl8-expressing macrophages drive immunosuppression",
    "Depleting CCR8⁺ regulatory T cells overcomes anti-PD-1 resistance in BRCA2-deficient tumors"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work identifies CCR8⁺ regulatory T cells as a key immunosuppressive mechanism in BRCA2-deficient prostate cancer, providing a target to overcome immunotherapy resistance in this aggressive subtype."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates whether brief dietary changes during acute inflammation influence long-term tissue immune memory. Using lean mice with early type 17 skin inflammation, researchers found that a transient high-fat diet (HFD) exposure within a 4-day window at disease onset exacerbates inflammation to levels seen in chronic obesity. HFD outside this window or switching obese mice to low-fat diet during the window reduces disease severity. Mechanistically, HFD rapidly recruits neutrophils, which express Nlrp3 and Il1b, driving pathogenic TH17 cell activation. Post-recovery, chromatin-poised tissue-resident memory T cells persist, causing exaggerated inflammation upon recurrence despite subsequent low-fat diet. The findings demonstrate that dietary state during a narrow temporal window can durably reprogram immune responses.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Transient high-fat diet exposure within a 4-day window at disease onset exacerbates acute type 17 skin inflammation to chronic obesity levels",
    "HFD outside this window or switching obese mice to low-fat diet during the window blunts disease amplification",
    "Neutrophil-mediated Nlrp3-Il1b signaling licenses pathogenic TH17 cells, with persistent chromatin-poised tissue-resident memory T cells after recovery"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study establishes that short-lived dietary states during acute inflammation can durably reprogram tissue immune memory via neutrophil-NLRP3-IL-1β signaling, revealing a temporal window for dietary interventions to modulate inflammatory disease trajectories."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates multi-ion discrimination in ultrathin polymeric membranes using aligned porphyrin pores. The researchers fabricated a membrane (~8.56 nm thick) via interfacial superspreading, achieving simultaneous separation of six ion species under microvolt-scale voltages. The membrane shows anomalous Li+ selectivity with a threshold voltage of ~400 mV, enabling >98% lithium recovery from brines at ultra-low energy consumption (1.1 × 1.0-4 Wh g-1).",
  "key_findings": [
    "Ultrathin polymeric membranes with angstrom-scale porphyrin pores achieve simultaneous multi-ion discrimination",
    "The membrane exhibits anomalous Li+ selectivity with a threshold voltage of ~400 mV",
    "Two-stage devices recover >98% lithium from brines at 1.1 × 10-4 Wh g-1 energy consumption"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a design principle for energy-efficient ion separation by leveraging threshold-voltage sieving in ultrathin membranes, enabling high selectivity for ions of similar charge and size without conventional energy penalties."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated whether clinical recovery in experimental periodontitis resets the local T cell repertoire. Using a mouse model with induced regulatory breakdown, researchers mapped gingival T cell dynamics across disease phases. They identified a clonally convergent CD4⁺ T cell population with Tfh/Tph-like transcriptional identity that persisted from recovery to recurrence. Single-cell analyses in human lesions confirmed a comparable population, suggesting a reservoir of antigen-experienced cells contributes to disease relapse.",
  "key_findings": [
    "A clonally convergent CD4⁺ T cell population with Tfh/Tph-like transcriptional identity persisted from recovery to recurrence in mice",
    "This population was detected in human periodontitis lesions via single-cell transcriptomics",
    "Clinical recovery did not reset the local T cell repertoire, indicating a reservoir of tissue-adapted antigen-experienced cells"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The findings establish a persistent reservoir of antigen-experienced CD4⁺ T cells that can drive periodontitis recurrence, revealing a mechanism for disease relapse beyond conventional immune memory."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates ionization-state-directed sulfonate engineering to enhance stability in inverted perovskite solar cells. Using sodium 2,2'-disulfanediyldiethanesulfonate (DDANA), the researchers created sulfonate-rich interfaces that form robust multisite binding with perovskite, optimize energy alignment, and scavenge corrosive iodine. The approach achieved 27.38% certified efficiency in cells, 25.88% in flexible devices, and 23.33% in modules, with >98% retention after 1000 hours of operation.",
  "key_findings": [
    "DDANA forms multisite binding with perovskite lattice via sulfonate groups",
    "Sulfonate-enriched interfaces optimize energy level alignment and hole transport",
    "DDANA-modified devices retain >98% efficiency after 1.000 hours of maximum power point tracking"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes sulfonate ionization-state engineering as a versatile strategy for developing chemically robust buried interfaces in perovskite solar cells, advancing stability and scalability without compromising efficiency."
}

{
  "summary": "This study developed a 31-parameter spectral flow cytometry panel to simultaneously profile immune cell subsets, functional cytokines (Th1, Th2, Th17), activation-induced markers (AIMs), and FoxP3+ regulatory T cells in human peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs). The panel enables deep immunophenotyping under steady-state and mitogen-stimulated conditions, facilitating comprehensive analysis of immune responses without prior antigen-specific stimulation. The approach addresses limitations of conventional methods by providing concurrent assessment of cytokine signatures and Treg functionality.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Simultaneous profiling of 31 immune parameters including cytokines, AIMs, and FoxP3+ Tregs in human PBMCs",
    "Validation of the panel for functional cytokine and AIMs assessment in mitogen-stimulated conditions",
    "Customizable design for antigen-specific immune response analysis"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This method enables precise, multiparametric immune profiling in human PBMCs, advancing the ability to dissect functional immune responses through concurrent cytokine, activation marker, and regulatory T cell analysis."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates thermal runaway mechanisms in lithium-ion batteries by identifying cathode-driven aluminothermic fronts as a heat-amplification pathway. Using semi-1D composite bars in argon, researchers isolated interfacial redox chemistry between nickel-rich layered oxide cathodes and aluminum. The aluminothermic fronts propagated at velocities comparable to thermal-runaway fronts, with propagation rates dependent on aluminum loading, cathode oxygen-donor capacity, and oxygen availability. Energy-audit calculations suggest this reaction releases heat equivalent to half the stored electrical energy, indicating a significant internal heat-amplification mechanism relevant to battery safety.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Aluminothermic fronts propagate at velocities matching thermal-runaway fronts in argon",
    "Propagation rates depend on aluminum loading, cathode oxygen-donor capacity, and oxygen availability",
    "The reaction releases heat equivalent to approximately half the stored electrical energy"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work identifies cathode-aluminum redox chemistry as a critical internal heat-amusing mechanism that accelerates thermal runaway in lithium-ion batteries, providing new insights for battery safety design."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated whether MHCII-expressing cancer-associated fibroblasts (CAFs) in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) promote antitumor immunity. Using orthotopic mouse models, researchers found that conventional CD4+ T cells increase MHCII+ CAFs via interferon gamma (IFNγ). Depleting regulatory T cells (Tregs) enhanced M. II+ CAF abundance, which suppressed tumor growth and metastasis. The results indicate MHCII+ CAFs can be harnessed for antitumor immune responses when Tregs are depleted.",
  "key_findings": [
    "MHCII+ CAFs increase in tumors following CD4+ T cell activation via IFNγ",
    "Treg depletion elevates MHCII+ CAF numbers, reducing tumor growth and metastasis",
    "MHCII expression on CAFs is essential for CD4+ T cell-mediated control of PDAC progression after Treg ablation"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates that MHCII-expressing CAFs can drive antitumor immunity in pancreatic cancer through IFNγ-dependent mechanisms, providing a potential target for immunotherapeutic strategies."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the synthesis of 2D perovskite heterostructures through sequential recrystallization. The approach involves a one-pot method to create diverse heterostructures by controlling crystallization sequences and material compositions. Results include high-quality heterostructures with multicolor emission and optical coupling. The findings enable tailored energy barriers and charge carrier control, advancing optoelectronic device design through precise band engineering.",
  "key_findings": [
    "High crystalline quality heterostructures fabricated via sequential recrystallization",
    "Multicolor emission properties observed across heterostructure regions",
    "Optical coupling between distinct heterostructure components"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a versatile strategy for designing 2D perovskite heterostructures with tunable electronic properties, enabling precise control over charge carrier dynamics for next-generation optoelectronic applications."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated whether locally engineered microorganisms could reprogram synovial metabolism to systemically re-educate immunity in rheumatoid arthritis. Using chemically attenuated photosynthetic bacteria modified for lactate metabolism and hydrogen production, researchers delivered bacteria intra-articularly to disrupt pathological immune crosstalk. Results showed restored Treg/Th1:7 balance locally, reduced circulating IL-6, and alleviated distant joint inflammation, indicating local metabolic reprogramming can systemically regulate immune responses without systemic immunosuppression.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Intra-articular delivery of engineered photosynthetic bacteria restored Treg/Th17 balance locally",
    "Circulating IL-6 levels decreased, indicating systemic immune regulation",
    "Local metabolic reprogramming alleviated distant joint inflammation"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates that targeted local metabolic reprogramming via engineered microorganisms can systemically re-educate immunity, providing a novel mechanism for treating autoimmune disorders without broad immunosuppression."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates enhancing the kinetics of polyiodide redox in aqueous zinc-iodine batteries to overcome shuttle effects and slow conversion rates. By integrating guanidinium cations into iodine cathodes, the researchers created interface-confined cationic polarization that electrostatically concentrates polyiodides and accelerates interfacial charge transfer. The optimized cathode achieved 100 mAh g-1 at 50 A g-1 and maintained 40,000 cycles with minimal capacity decay, demonstrating high-rate performance and long cycle life without irreversible trapping.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Interface-confined guanidinium cations enable electrostatic polyiodide enrichment in cathodes",
    "Cation-π interactions with Ketjen Black carbon domains stabilize the cationic polarization",
    "The cathode achieves 100 mAh g-1 at 50 A g-1 with 40,000 cycles and 0.002% per-cycle decay"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This approach provides a novel mechanism for rapid and reversible polyiodide conversion in aqueous zinc-iodine batteries, advancing high-rate and long-life energy storage systems."
}

{
  "summary": "This systematic evidence map analyzed 222 primary studies on osteoporosis extracellular vesicles to identify research concentrations across osteoimmune mechanisms, mechanistic depth, and human evidence. It found 90.5% used animal models, 51.8% employed advanced mechanistic perturbation, and only 16.2% included human data. The remodeling interface was the most studied module, while few studies connected donor states to osteoimmune mediators and human outcomes.",
  "key_findings": [
    "90.5% of studies used animal models, with only 16.2% linking to human data",
    "51.8% of studies applied advanced mechanistic perturbation, but 84 studies lacked human evidence",
    "The remodeling interface module comprised the largest group (n=120) of studies"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This evidence map clarifies gaps in translating osteoporosis extracellular vesicle research from mechanistic models to human applications by identifying underutilized study designs and untested pathways connecting donor states to skeletal outcomes."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates creating biodegradable nanofibers with dual antimicrobial activity and electrostatic particle capture. Researchers covalently attached sodium copper chlorophyllin (SCC) and quaternary ammonium compounds (QAC) to silk nanofibers. The design enabled intramolecular charge separation in SCC, enhancing visible-light-driven reactive oxygen species production, while QAC provided cationic surfaces for microbial inactivation and nanoparticle capture. The fibers demonstrated potent antibacterial/antiviral efficacy under both dark and light conditions, maintained biodegradability in soil and enzymatic environments, and achieved high capture efficiency without compromising sustainability.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Covalent immobilization of SCC and QAC on silk nanofibers achieved dual antimicrobial activity under dark and visible light conditions",
    "Intramolecular charge separation in SCC suppressed electron-hole recombination, amplifying reactive oxygen species generation under visible light",
    "QAC imparted permanent cationic charges enabling contact-mediated microbial inactivation and humidity-resistant electrostatic particle capture"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a molecular design strategy for sustainable, biodegradable nanofibers that simultaneously integrate antimicrobial efficacy, electrostatic particle capture, and environmental degradability through surface co-functionalization."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how cellular mechanics influence regulatory T cell (Treg) immune suppression mechanisms. It proposes that membrane tension and actin organization govern Treg efficiency in pMHCII and costimulatory ligand removal via trogocytosis and trans-endocytosis. The research identifies a biophysical signature for antigen-specific suppression, highlighting mechanics as a key regulator of Treg function.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Treg efficiency in pM:HCII and costimulatory ligand removal depends on membrane tension and actin organization",
    "Mechanics govern Treg-mediated antigen-specific suppression through trogocytosis and trans-endocytosis",
    "A distinct biophysical signature emerges for Treg immune suppression mechanisms"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study establishes cellular mechanics as a fundamental regulator of regulatory T cell function, providing a biophysical framework for understanding immune suppression mechanisms."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how a rigid double-spiro-grid polymer (NPSG) regulates donor fibrillar pre-assembly and acceptor interdiffusion in layer-by-layer organic solar cells. By introducing NPSG as a donor underlayer, the research demonstrates enhanced fibrillar organization of D18, moderated L8-BO interdiffusion, and formation of a vertically graded heterojunction. These morphological improvements lead to a power conversion efficiency of 21.2% with improved stability and reproducibility in sequentially processed devices.",
  "key_findings": [
    "NPSG promotes pronounced fibrillar pre-assemblies of D18 in solution",
    "The rigid NPSG backbone moderates L8-BO interdiffusion through a coherent donor framework",
    "Optimized devices achieve 21.2% power conversion efficiency with enhanced stability and reproducibility"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes donor-underlayer pre-assembly regulation as a general strategy for achieving high-efficiency and stable layer-by-layer organic solar cells through controlled morphology engineering."
}

{
  "summary": "This case report investigates IgG4-related peric: a 62-year-old woman with dyspnea due to recurrent right pleural effusion and pericardial thickening. Pericardial biopsy revealed lymphoplasmacytic infiltration with high IgG4/IgG ratio and abundant IgG4-positive plasma cells. FOXP3-positive cells within lymphoid follicles suggested regulatory T-cell involvement. The patient responded to prednisolone, highlighting the role of Tfr cells in pericardial pathology when pleural and pericardial lesions coexist.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Pericardial biopsy demonstrated lymphoplasmacytic infiltration with IgG4/IgG ratio >85% and 63.3 IgG4-positive plasma cells per high-power field",
    "Numerous FOXP3-positive cells were identified within lymphoid follicles, indicating regulatory T-cell involvement",
    "Prednisolone treatment led to marked reduction of pleural effusion in IgG4-related pericarditis"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This case provides evidence of Tfr cell-mediated immune mechanisms in IgG4-related pericarditis, offering insights into the pathophysiology of IgG4-related disease in pericardial contexts."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates engineering acoustic topological textures using moiré superlattices. Researchers created a phase-controlled spoof surface acoustic wave platform on a perforated acoustic metasusing two twisted skyrmion lattices superposed to form controllable acoustic skyrmion bags. The twist angle and rotation center dictated bag scale and geometry, while controlled defects demonstrated enhanced stability of skyrmion clusters at higher defect fractions compared to untwisted lattices. The platform also enables transitions between skyrmion and meron lattices.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Two twisted skyrmion lattices superposed to form controllable acoustic skyrmion bags",
    "Twist angle and rotation center control bag scale and geometry",
    "Enhanced stability of skyrmion clusters at higher defect fractions compared to untwisted lattices"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes acoustic moiré superlattices as a reconfigurable platform for engineering robust real-space topological textures, advancing topological acoustics through programmable topological textures in wave fields."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated whether Resolvin D1 (RvD1) could ameliorate autoimmune thyroiditis (AIT) in NOD.H-2h4 mice by modulating Th17/Treg balance. Mice with induced AIT received RvD1 at low or high doses via intraperitoneal injection. Results showed high-dose RvD1 significantly reduced thyroglobulin antibodies, improved thyroid indices, decreased inflammation, and restored Th17/Treg balance, while low-dose treatment showed numerical improvements without statistical significance.",
  "key_findings": [
    "High-dose RvD1 significantly decreased thyroglobulin antibody levels in AIT mice",
    "RvD1 treatment reduced histological inflammation and pro-inflammatory cytokines",
    "RvD1 decreased splenic Th17 proportions and increased Treg proportions, lowering the Th17/Treg ratio"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This study demonstrates that RvD1 can restore immune balance in autoimmune thyroiditis, providing a potential immunomodulatory strategy for managing this condition."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses the challenge of achieving long-range order in 2D conjugated polymer single crystals by decoupling monomer diffusion from chain propagation via gas-solid interfacial polymerization. The approach uses surface coordination to activate phenylacetylene and π-stacking-driven assembly, suppressing chain entanglement. It produced micrometer-scale PPA single-crystalline nanoribbons with helical chains and long-range crystallinity, achieving a molecular weight up to ∼3.7 × 109 g mol-1 and efficient photodetection across 375–1550 nm.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Gas-solid interfacial polymerization enables quasi-2D polyphenylacetylene single-crystalline nanoribbons with long-range crystallinity",
    "The nanoribbons exhibit helical chain structures and a molecular weight up to ∼3.7 × 109 g mol-1",
    "Photodetection capabilities from 375 to 1550 nm with high responsivity (30.1 A W-1) and detectivity (8.65 × 1012 Jones)"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a strategy to decouple molecular-weight growth from structural order in conjugated polymers, enabling high-performance quasi-2D polymer semiconductors for optoelectronic applications."
}

{
  "summary": "This review examines mesenchymal stromal cells (MSCs) and their derivatives as potential therapies for inflammatory bowel diseases (IBD). It highlights MSCs' immunomodulatory mechanisms, including suppression of effector T-cells, expansion of regulatory T and B cells, and anti-inflammatory macrophage polarization. The study emphasizes paracrine signaling via secretome and extracellular vesicles as key mediators of therapeutic effects. Clinical progress is noted for perianal fistulas, with cell-free approaches showing promise for improved safety and scalability in IBD treatment.",
  "key_findings": [
    "MSCs modulate intestinal inflammation through immunomodulatory mechanisms such as T-cell suppression and regulatory cell expansion",
    "Paracrine signals from MSCs, including secretome and extracellular vesicles, drive tissue repair and anti-inflammatory effects",
    "Cell-free MSC-derived therapies demonstrate improved safety and scalability compared to direct cell transplantation"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work advances understanding of MSC-based therapeutic mechanisms in IBD, providing a foundation for developing safer, scalable cell-free strategies to modulate intestinal inflammation."
}

{
  "summary": "This review investigates heteroatomic active sites for electrochemical CO2 reduction to multicarbon products. It evaluates theoretical design, precise synthesis methods, and mechanistic insights into how heteroatoms enhance selectivity and efficiency beyond single-metal catalysts. The study identifies key strategies for overcoming linear scaling relations and improving energy efficiency in COxRR toward C2+ products.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Theoretical modeling identifies high-performance heteroatomic sites through descriptor-based approaches",
    "Precision synthesis enables atomically dispersed and interfacial-confined heter:atomic structures",
    "Heteroatoms improve selectivity via synergistic electronic and geometric modulation"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a systematic framework for designing heteroatomic catalysts that overcome fundamental limitations in electrochemical CO2 reduction to multicarbon products, advancing sustainable carbon utilization pathways."
}

{
  "summary": "This review examines radiation-induced intestinal injury (RIII) as a dose-limiting toxicity of abdominopelvic radiotherapy. It investigates how immune microenvironment dynamics drive injury progression, including DAMP-mediated innate activation via TLR4/NLRP3/cGAS-STING, neutrophil and M.1 macrophage-driven inflammation, and Th1/Th17 polarization. The study identifies key mechanisms for chronic fibrosis and vascular sclerosis, emphasizing the need for risk-stratified biomarkers and strategies to decouple gut protection from antitumor immunity during radiotherapy.",
  "key_findings": [
    "DAMPs from damaged epithelia activate innate immunity through TLR4/NLRP3/cGAS-STING pathways",
    "Neutrophils and M1 macrophages sustain TNF-necroptosis-DAMP inflammatory loops",
    "Balanced macrophage repolarization, Treg expansion, and IL-22 promote resolution of inflammation"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study elucidates stage-dependent immune mechanisms in radiation-induced intestinal injury, providing a foundation for developing targeted strategies to mitigate normal tissue damage while preserving antitumor immune responses during radiotherapy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates charge-driven polymorphic phase transitions in Mo0.92W0.08Te2 for multilevel memristive devices. Using electrostatic coupling to a ferroionic substrate (CuInP2S6), the researchers achieved reversible switching between semiconducting and metallic phases with low energy barriers. The approach enabled nonvolatile, programmable phase control, yielding multiple stable conductance states via phase-dependent Schottky junctions. The method demonstrated 98.45% accuracy in MNIST inference, highlighting its potential for neuromorphic applications.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Reversible switching between semiconducting and metallic phases via electrostatically accumulated charges",
    "Nonvolatile charge accumulation enabled by ferroionic substrate coupling",
    "Multiple stable conductance states achieved through phase-dependent Schottky junction evolution"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a charge-driven mechanism for polymorphic phase engineering in memristive devices, providing a pathway to multilevel nonvolatile resistance modulation without traditional phase-change barriers."
}
